  "article": {
    "hero": {
      "title": "🖋️ Tattoo Shop Name Generator",
      "tagline": "Names for a tattoo shop, studio or private tattoo artist."
    },
    "intro": "Good tattoo shop names have a bit of character and say something about your style: traditional symbols for an old-school shop, clean words for a fine-line studio. The **Tattoo Shop Name Generator** suggests names for a tattoo shop, tattoo studio or parlour (parlor in the US): catchy, funny, cute and fine line, traditional, cool, modern, your town or your own name.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "Eight good tattoo shop names",
        "content": "<p>All eight came from the generator:</p><ul><li><strong>Heart & Mermaid Tattoo</strong>: two classic flash designs, the traditional way.</li><li><strong>Panther & Lighthouse Tattoo</strong>: traditional symbols with a story behind them.</li><li><strong>Forge Lane Tattooing</strong>: a classic street-style name.</li><li><strong>Line & Shade Tattoo</strong>: catchy, and names the craft.</li><li><strong>Seedling Fine Line Tattoo</strong>: soft and small, for delicate work.</li><li><strong>Steel Swallow Tattoo</strong>: a cool take on a traditional sailor's tattoo.</li><li><strong>Monoline Tattoo</strong>: modern and minimal.</li><li><strong>Worth the Wait Tattoo Club</strong>: funny, and true of every good tattoo.</li></ul>"
      },
      {
        "heading": "How tattoo shop names work",
        "content": "<p>Traditional Western tattooing grew up around sailors' tattoos, and many classic designs carry a meaning. A swallow was said to bring a sailor home, an anchor stands for steadiness, and a panther or tiger for strength. Those designs make good names because they suggest your style before anyone sees your portfolio.</p><ul><li><strong>Traditional</strong> names pair two flash designs: <em>Clover & Eagle Tattoo</em>.</li><li><strong>Cute and fine line</strong> names are small and soft: <em>Wren Ink</em>, <em>Little Snowdrop Tattoo</em>.</li><li><strong>Cool</strong> names add metal, fire or animals: <em>Iron Serpent Tattoo</em>.</li><li><strong>Modern</strong> names use one design word: <em>Etch & Co.</em>, <em>Sketch Collective</em>.</li></ul><p>Studio, parlour and shop all work. \"Collective\" suits a shop with several resident artists.</p>"
      },
      {
        "heading": "Check it's free before you print the sign",
        "content": "<p>Press <strong>Check</strong> on any name, or paste one into the <a href=\"../business-name-checker.html\">Business Name Checker</a>, to see whether the domain and social handles are free and to jump to the company register and trade mark searches. Then:</p><ul><li><strong>Search the company register.</strong> In the UK that's Companies House; in the US, your state's business register.</li><li><strong>Search trade marks</strong> with the UK Intellectual Property Office or the USPTO, especially if you'll sell products or open more than one site.</li><li><strong>Look locally.</strong> Search Google Maps for tattoo studios near you: a similar name a few streets away will confuse customers even if it's legally fine.</li><li><strong>Register with the council.</strong> In the UK, tattooists and their premises must be registered or licensed with the local council before they start work, and tattooing anyone under 18 is illegal.</li></ul><p>This is general guidance, not legal advice. For more on choosing and checking a name, read our guide: <a href=\"../guides/business-naming-best-practices.html\">Business Naming Best Practices</a>.</p>"
      },
